Jahshaka patch 0037 — VctLighting::setVoxelizer, plus three lifetime defects in
the pin that a cascade chain makes reachable.

SOURCE patch. EVERY TREE MUST RE-RUN `irisgl/scripts/build-ogre.sh` after
pulling this (reset the ogre-next submodule first if it already carries an
earlier revision of any patch: `git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next checkout -- .
&& git -C irisgl/thirdparty/ogre-next clean -fd`).

WHY A PATCH AND NOT A WORKAROUND (owner rule, 2026-09-14, ledger §325). The first
cut of Photon's per-cascade dirty path (PHOTON_SPEC G1) reached into VctLighting's
`protected` members from a derived class of ours to point a live lighting at a
replacement voxelizer. That works, but it is a private copy of a decision that
belongs to the engine: the hook is missing upstream, and one of the three
defects below is a defect in the pin whoever hits it next will hit again. So the
hook is added where it belongs and the defects are fixed at their source.

WHAT THE HOOK IS FOR. `VctMaterial` converts each datablock ONCE and caches the
result by RAW POINTER for the voxelizer's whole life, so a material whose
parameters changed — or one whose datablock died and whose address may since have
been recycled — can only be answered by a NEW VctVoxelizer. Destroying the
VctLighting with it is not an option inside a cascade chain: `addCascade()` hands
every cascade inside this one a raw pointer to it and `fillConstBufferData()`
walks them on EVERY pass, so replacing one cascade's lighting forces the whole
chain to be rebuilt in a single frame — the hitch the chain's one-rebuild-per-
frame budget exists to prevent. `setVoxelizer()` moves the lighting to the
replacement in place: the TextureGpuListener registrations move to the new
albedo/normal textures and the light voxels are re-created from the new
resolution — the same two steps the pin already performs when a voxelizer loses
residency, driven on purpose instead of by a device loss.

THE THREE DEFECTS, all of them in the pin as shipped:

  1. `mVoxelizerTexturesChanged` IS NEVER CLEARED. It is raised in
     `notifyTextureChanged` (LostResidency / Deleted), read in `checkTextures()`
     and `resetTexturesFromBuildRelative()`, and assigned `false` only in the
     constructor. So a voxelizer that loses residency once makes every later
     `update()` destroy and re-create the light voxel textures, for the object's
     whole life. `checkTextures()` now clears it after serving it.

  2. `correct_area_light_shadows` IS WRITTEN ON A SHARED JOB FROM ONE
     VOXELIZER'S MIPS. "VCT/LightInjection" is found by name, so every
     VctLighting in the process holds the SAME HlmsComputeJob, while the property
     describes the voxelizer being injected (whether its albedo carries the mips
     the correction reads). `createTextures()` writes it, so with more than one
     VctLighting alive the value in force is whichever one created its textures
     last. Upstream's own `VctCascadedVoxelizer` builds its cascades in order at
     construction and never touches a voxelizer again, so the last cascade's
     value simply stands for the whole chain and nothing re-asserts it; the
     moment one cascade is re-voxelized on its own the chain injects the wrong
     value until the next full build. `update()` now re-asserts the property from
     the voxelizer this lighting actually samples, per injection.
     `HlmsComputeJob::setProperty` only invalidates the PSO cache hash when the
     value changes, so re-asserting the same value costs nothing.

  3. `IrradianceFieldRaster::destroyWorkspace` DROPS TWO OF ITS THREE
     WORKSPACES. `createWorkspace()` makes `mRenderWorkspace`,
     `mConvertToIfdWorkspace` and `mIfdIntegrationWorkspace`, all three against
     `mCamera`; the teardown removes the first two and then destroys the camera,
     leaving a live compositor workspace holding a dangling `Camera*`. It is
     reachable today (a raster irradiance field whose workspaces are dropped and
     re-created around a shadow-atlas rebuild — PHOTON_SPEC G2) and it leaks one
     such workspace per round.

Verified: `git apply --check` against the pristine pin and `git apply --reverse
--check` after applying; `build-ogre.sh` re-run in the lane worktree; the
`--engine-selftest` hash is unchanged (`b55e2d5d…`) — nothing here changes a
pixel in a scene with one voxel volume and no raster field.

